; deny_keychain.sb
;
; Seatbelt (sandbox-exec) profile used by neutralize_credentials.sh to run a
; single subprocess (and everything it forks/execs) with macOS Keychain
; access categorically denied, while leaving everything else about that
; subprocess's environment alone -- filesystem, network, process exec, etc.
; all still work normally, per (allow default) below. Only Keychain reads/
; writes and the IPC path Keychain access actually goes over are removed.
;
; WHY THIS FILE EXISTS:
; Unsetting a credential environment variable is not sufficient to neutralize
; a credential. Many CLI tools resolve an API key from an environment variable
; FIRST and fall back to a platform credential store when it is absent -- and
; that fallback path does not consult environment variables at all. So a test
; harness that unsets the env var and believes it has produced a
; credential-free subprocess can still hand that subprocess a live key, which
; then reaches wherever the subprocess writes: logs, transcripts, artifacts.
;
; This profile addresses the fallback half. It makes the Keychain unreachable
; to the wrapped subprocess tree, so there is no fallback left to fall back
; to. Use it together with the env-var unset in neutralize_credentials.sh;
; neither half is sufficient alone.
;
; WHY A DENY-ONLY PROFILE ON TOP OF (allow default), NOT A SCRATCH KEYCHAIN:
; An earlier design-time recommendation assumed a
; separate scratch keychain (security create-keychain + a temporary search
; list) would be the correct mechanism. Building the real thing surfaced a
; strictly safer alternative: a scratch keychain requires creating a new
; keychain file and mutating the process's keychain SEARCH LIST (even if
; scoped to a subshell/subprocess env, `security list-keychains -s` is a
; global-per-user-session operation in some invocation forms, and getting
; that scoping wrong risks the real login keychain silently dropping off the
; search list or a new keychain silently becoming default). This profile
; touches, creates, unlocks, and modifies NOTHING about the user's real
; keychain or its search list -- it only denies one subprocess tree the
; ability to reach Keychain services at all. A profile that can't read
; anything can't leak anything, and there is no shared mutable state for a
; concurrent trial to race against or for a bug in this profile to corrupt.
;
; HOW TO VERIFY IT ON YOUR OWN TOOL, and the one trap in doing so:
; Run your tool's own "is a key present" check three ways -- normal
; environment; credential env var unset with no sandbox; env var unset UNDER
; this profile. Only the third should report no key. If the second also
; reports no key, your tool has no credential-store fallback and this profile
; is not doing anything for you.
;
; The trap: under this profile a Keychain query fails with an EXTRA
; "SecKeychainSearchCreateFromAttributes: One or more parameters passed to a
; function were not valid" error before the generic not-found message. A
; query for an item that simply does not exist produces only the generic
; message, with no such prefix. Check for the prefix. Without it you cannot
; distinguish "the sandbox denied this" from "the item was never there," and
; a test that cannot tell those apart will pass whether or not the profile
; works.
;
; This profile is intentionally coarse: it denies ALL Keychain access for
; the wrapped subprocess tree, not just the one named item a target declares
; via api_key_keychain. A named-item-only deny would require trusting that
; no OTHER Keychain item could also satisfy whatever the target's fallback
; logic is looking for -- a blanket deny needs no such trust.

(version 1)
(allow default)

; HOME_KEYCHAINS is supplied by neutralize_credentials.sh via sandbox-exec -D.
; It is a parameter rather than a literal for two reasons: the caller's real home
; directory should not be committed to this repo, and a hardcoded placeholder path
; here would match nothing at runtime -- the rule would read as protective while
; denying access to a directory that does not exist. A guard that silently covers
; the wrong path is worse than no guard, because it still reads as covered.
(deny file-read* file-write*
  (subpath (param "HOME_KEYCHAINS"))
  (subpath "/Library/Keychains")
  (subpath "/System/Library/Keychains")
  (subpath "/private/var/db/mds"))

(deny mach-lookup
  (global-name "com.apple.securityd")
  (global-name "com.apple.securityd.xpc")
  (global-name "com.apple.security.agent")
  (global-name "com.apple.SecurityServer")
  (global-name "com.apple.trustd")
  (global-name "com.apple.trustd.agent")
  (global-name "com.apple.ocspd")
  (global-name "com.apple.security.keychain-circle.notification"))
